---
// The docs front page: a short overview that says what each page covers and
// where to start. The sidebar already lists every page, so this doesn't.
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
// releases.md arrives with the release it describes, so it is linked only
// once a published nightly has shipped it (as the sidebar does).
const hasReleases = (await getCollection('docs')).some((d) => d.id === 'releases');
---

<Docs
  title="Docs"
  fullTitle="Pulsar docs: install, updates, gaming and agents"
  description="How to install and use Pulsar, with pages on themes, games, coding agents, updates and rollback, troubleshooting, and how each image is built and signed."
  path="/docs"
>
  <h1>Pulsar docs</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    If you’re new to Pulsar, start with <a href="/docs/install">Install</a>. If you’re on Windows or
    a Mac now, read <a href="/docs/coming-from">From Windows or macOS</a> first. It starts with which
    computers can run Pulsar, then shows how to keep Windows on the same disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system is a read-only image. Apps install as Flatpaks, dev tools go in a toolbox and services
    run as quadlets. <a href="/docs/where-things-go">Where things go</a> has the commands for each.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar theme set</code> switches the whole desktop to one of twenty-four{' '}
    <a href="/docs/themes">themes</a>, and <a href="/docs/theming">How theming works</a> lists what
    each piece of a theme writes. Steam, Heroic and the other{' '}
    <a href="/docs/gaming">game launchers</a> install on first boot. <a href="/docs/gamescale">gamescale</a>{' '}
    runs each game at your screen’s real resolution while fractional scaling is on. On the NVIDIA
    image, a podman container gets the GPU with <code>--device nvidia.com/gpu=all</code>, which is how
    you run <a href="/docs/gpu-containers">CUDA and local models</a> without layering anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    To install Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, opencode or aider, or run one on a local model, see{' '}
    <a href="/docs/agents">Coding agents</a>. <a href="/docs/agents-safety">Agents and safety</a>{' '}
    lists what an agent can change without asking you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Updates download in the background and apply when you restart. To go back to the previous build,
    see <a href="/docs/updates">Updates and rollback</a>. When something breaks, run{' '}
    <code>pulsar doctor</code>, then read <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>. Every
    subcommand is in <a href="/docs/cli">The pulsar command</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each image carries an SPDX SBOM and is signed with Pulsar’s release key.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/provenance">Provenance</a> shows how to check both, and{' '}
    <a href="/docs/signing">Signing</a> lists both of Pulsar’s keys, including the one that signs the
    NVIDIA kernel module.
    {hasReleases && (
      <>
        {' '}The <a href="/docs/releases">release notes</a> list what’s new in each Pulsar release.
      </>
    )}{' '}
    <a href="/docs/changelog">Nightly builds</a> lists every package that changed in the latest build.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .intro {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    max-width: 56ch;
  }
</style>
